{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t09-005",
  "track": "track-09",
  "title": "Linux shell permissions processes networking systemd and logs",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "A Linux command runs with a process identity, environment, working directory, file descriptors, and permissions. Shell syntax composes commands, while exit status and standard streams provide the basic control and diagnostic contract.",
    "Filesystem access combines ownership, mode bits, ACLs, directory search permission, mount behavior, and process credentials. Use the least authority needed and understand that sudo changes privilege for a command; it does not make unsafe paths or copied commands safe.",
    "Processes have lifecycle states, parent-child relationships, signals, resource limits, and namespaces. A zombie is an exited child awaiting reaping, not a running process that SIGKILL can remove; inspect evidence before terminating anything.",
    "systemd models services and other resources as units with dependency and ordering relationships. `enable` configures activation links while `start` changes current runtime state; unit security controls, restart policy, and logs must be interpreted in the context of the installed systemd version and distribution.",
    "Linux network troubleshooting follows layers: interface/link, address, route, DNS, socket listener, firewall, TLS, and application. A successful ping does not prove that a TCP listener, DNS name, certificate, or HTTP route works.",
    "The journal is structured, indexed systemd log storage, while traditional files and application logs may exist alongside it. Query by unit, boot, priority, time, and cursor; preserve timestamps and permissions when collecting incident evidence."
  ],
